      <div class="text-element">
  
      <p>Planning an epic Iron Man costume for Halloween or Comic Con, or looking for that iconic piece that turns a plain t-shirt into Tony Stark? Look no further, for in this guide we'll show you how to make your own electronic glowing reactor with a cool pulsing effect. <br><br>You can even customize it once complete, go for red, purple, green, pink: Whatever color will power you up! Or change the pulse rate or effects to add a special touch.</p>
    
</div>

      <div class="alert-element alert-info" role="note">
  <span class="alert-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="50" height="50" viewBox="0 0 50 50" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <path d="M25 50a25 25 0 1 0 0-50 25 25 0 0 0 0 50zm0-37.5a2.338 2.338 0 0 1 2.344 2.344V25.78A2.338 2.338 0 0 1 25 28.125a2.338 2.338 0 0 1-2.344-2.344V14.844A2.338 2.338 0 0 1 25 12.5zm-3.125 21.875a3.125 3.125 0 1 1 6.25 0 3.125 3.125 0 0 1-6.25 0z" fill="currentColor"/>
</svg>
</span>
  <span class="visually-hidden">Text emphasized with a blue exclamation: </span>
  <span class="alert-content">This guide was written for the 'original' Gemma board, but can be done with either the original or M0 Gemma. We recommend the Gemma M0 as it is easier to use and is more compatible with modern computers!</span>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
      <p>This is a simple soldering+crafts project, but before you begin, please review the following prerequisite guides:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<a href="../http/learn.adafruit.com/introducing-gemma">Introducing GEMMA</a>&nbsp;or <a href="./adafruit-gemma-m0">Introducing Gemma M0</a>
</li>
<li><a href="./adafruit-neopixel-uberguide">Adafruit NeoPixel Überguide</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Vector designs by Dano Wall. Vice photo by John de Cristofaro. <br><br>Parts list:</p>
<ul>
